\begin_layout Subsection
Flux coordinate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we want to express the magnetic field as :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{B}=\nabla\psi\times\nabla\varphi\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Where 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is the poloidal flux coordinate and 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi$
\end_inset

 the normal azimuth angle for spherical 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\vartheta,\varphi\right)$
\end_inset

 or toroidal 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(r,\theta,\varphi\right)$
\end_inset

 coordinates.
 We know the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{B}$
\end_inset

, so the equation reads :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\frac{2\cos\vartheta}{\rho^{3}}\hat{\rho}+\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\frac{\sin\vartheta}{\rho^{3}}\hat{\vartheta}=\nabla\psi\times\frac{1}{\rho\sin\vartheta}\hat{\varphi}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{1}{\rho}\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\vartheta}\hat{\rho}-\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\rho}\hat{\vartheta}=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\left(\frac{2\cos\vartheta\sin\vartheta}{\rho^{2}}\hat{\rho}+\frac{\sin^{2}\vartheta}{\rho^{2}}\hat{\vartheta}\right)\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can therefore deduce the expression of the flux coordinate with the integrati
on constant arbitrarily chosen at 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(R_{p},\frac{\pi}{2},0\right)$
\end_inset

 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\psi\left(\rho,\vartheta\right)=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\frac{\sin^{2}\vartheta}{\rho}}\label{eq:psidipolesph}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 is constant along the magnetic field lines 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\rho_{0}\sin^{2}\vartheta$
\end_inset

, which is consistent with the magnetic equilibrium.
 There, the expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

 reads :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\psi\left(\rho_{0}\right)=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi\rho_{0}}}\label{eq:psidipolesphrho0}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Representation of a magnetic flux surface is shown on figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Magnetic-dipole-flux"

\end_inset

.
 The expression of the magnetic flux can also be calculated using the flux
 of the magnetic dipole field through the cone 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\left[0,\rho\right],\vartheta,\left[0,2\pi\right]\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We can then write :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
2\pi\psi=\int_{0}^{\rho}\int_{0}^{2\pi}\left(-B_{\vartheta}\right)\rho\sin\vartheta d\varphi d\rho=2\pi\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\frac{\sin^{2}\vartheta}{\rho}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We obtain the same expression as 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:psidipolesph"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

