\begin_layout Subsection
Influence of Earth's radius on particle motion
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have considered until now a magnetic dipole without any interference
 whatsoever.
 However, particles that enter contact with Earth's lower atmosphere dissipate
 their energy into it and can be considered lost, since no longer trapped
 in the magnetic field.
 Let us call 
\begin_inset Formula $R_{T}$
\end_inset

 this minimum distance and 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{r_{0}}{R_{T}}$
\end_inset

 the ratio of the particle's equatorial distance to the center of the Earth
 compared to this minimum distance.
 For the particle to remain trapped, it will need to observe the following
 equation :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
r_{0}\sin^{2}\vartheta_{b}-R_{T}>0\Leftrightarrow a\sin^{2}\vartheta_{b}-1>0\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta_{b}$
\end_inset

 depends only on 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}$
\end_inset

, we can therefore determine the values 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\xi_{0},a\right)$
\end_inset

 for which the particles remain trapped.
 We can already see that if 
\begin_inset Formula $a<1$
\end_inset

, the particles are always lost, which is physically consistent.
 The limit equation can be expressed as :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\sin^{2}\vartheta_{b}=\frac{1}{a}\Leftrightarrow\cos^{2}\vartheta_{b}=1-\frac{1}{a}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can therefore deduce the evolution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0max}$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\xi_{0max}=\sqrt{1-\frac{1}{a^{3}\sqrt{4-\frac{3}{a}}}}}\label{eq:xi0max}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The trends are shown on figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Earth-radius-limit"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

