\begin_layout Subsection
Bounce time scale in a dipole magnetic field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In spherical 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\vartheta,\varphi\right)$
\end_inset

 coordinates the equation of a dipole magnetic field line is :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho\left(\theta\right)=\rho_{0}\sin^{2}\vartheta\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{0}$
\end_inset

 is the distance from the magnetic dipole at the equator, i.e.
 at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta=\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 To calculate the length of a field line, we start from the expression of
 the infinitesimal field line coordinate step :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
ds^{2}=d\rho^{2}+\left(\rho d\vartheta\right)^{2}=\left(\rho'^{2}+\rho^{2}\right)d\vartheta^{2}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
ds^{2}=\rho_{0}^{2}\sin^{2}\vartheta\left(4\cos^{2}\vartheta+\sin^{2}\vartheta\right)d\vartheta^{2}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
ds=\rho_{0}\sin\vartheta\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}d\vartheta\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can therefore deduce the bounce period expression, the velocity along
 the field line being 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{\parallel}=v\xi$
\end_inset

 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\tau_{b}=\frac{2}{v}\int_{L}\frac{ds}{\xi}=2\frac{\rho_{0}}{v}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\pi-\vartheta_{b}}\frac{\sin\vartheta\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\xi\left(\vartheta\right)}d\vartheta=4\frac{\rho_{0}}{v}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\frac{\sin\vartheta\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\xi\left(\vartheta\right)}d\vartheta\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last expression comes from the symmetry of the problem with respect
 to the equatorial plane (
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta=\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

).
 Using 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xi"

\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:bdipoleamplfieldline"

\end_inset

, the formal expression for the bounce time scale becomes :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_{b}=4\frac{\rho_{0}}{v}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\frac{\sin\vartheta\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\sqrt{1-\frac{\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\sin^{6}\vartheta}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)}}d\vartheta\label{eq:taub}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\tau_{b}=4\frac{\rho_{0}}{v}f\left(\xi_{0}\right)}\label{eq:taubf}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
since we have seen that 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta_{b}$
\end_inset

 depends only from 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 trend is shown in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:bounce-time-scale"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename taubxi0.eps
	width 100line%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Bounce time scale function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:bounce-time-scale"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can therefore normalize the bounce time scale as follows :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_{bn}=\frac{\tau_{b}}{\tau_{b}^{*}}=\frac{\tau_{b}}{4\frac{\rho_{0}}{v}f\left(0\right)}=\frac{f\left(\xi_{0}\right)}{f\left(0\right)}\label{eq:taubn}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Earth's ionosphere spans from 
\begin_inset Formula $150km$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}=800km$
\end_inset

 in altitude.
 The planet's average radius is 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{T}=6370km$
\end_inset

.
 A particle entering the ionosphere with 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}=1$
\end_inset

 and moving at a tenth of the speed of light will have a maximum bounce
 period between North and South poles of :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_{b}\simeq\frac{4\times1.38\left(6370\times10^{3}+800\times10^{3}\right)}{0.3\times10^{8}}=1.32s\label{eq:taubmax}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is not taking into account the 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta_{b}$
\end_inset

 limit due to the Earth radius, so the bounce time scale will in practise
 be a little smaller, but still remain in the same ordering.
 We can also note here that this calculation is done regardless of the particle
 mass or charge, and also regardless of the magnetic dipole strength.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Drift time scale in a dipole magnetic field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a magnetic field, the drift velocity due to the field gradient and the
 field line curvature has the following expression :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{v}_{d}=\frac{mv^{2}}{2qB}\left(1+\xi^{2}\right)\frac{\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B}{B^{2}}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To calculate this velocity, we fist need to evaluate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B}{B^{3}}$
\end_inset

 in spherical 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\rho,\vartheta,\varphi\right)$
\end_inset

 coordinates, with the expressions of the magnetic field and field gradient
 previously calculated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B=3\left(\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\right)^{2}\frac{1}{\rho^{7}}\left(-\frac{2\cos^{2}\vartheta\sin\vartheta}{\sqrt{3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1}}+\sin\vartheta\sqrt{3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1}\right)\hat{\varphi}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B=3\left(\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}\right)^{2}\frac{1}{\rho^{7}}\frac{\left(1-\cos^{2}\vartheta\right)\sin\vartheta}{\sqrt{3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1}}\hat{\varphi}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\frac{\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B}{B^{3}}=\frac{4\pi\rho^{2}}{\mu_{0}M}\frac{3\sin^{3}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}\hat{\varphi}\label{eq:bgradbdipole}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we take into account that the particle in collisionless motion will
 remain on its magnetic surface, therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho=\rho_{0}\sin^{2}\vartheta$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{0}$
\end_inset

 being the equatorial radius of the particle position at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vartheta=\frac{\pi}{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The expression becomes :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{\mathbf{B}\times\nabla B}{B^{3}}=\frac{4\pi\rho_{0}^{2}}{\mu_{0}M}\frac{3\sin^{7}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}\hat{\varphi}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\mathbf{v}_{d}=\frac{mv^{2}}{q}\frac{6\pi\rho_{0}^{2}}{\mu_{0}M}\left(1+\xi^{2}\right)\frac{\sin^{7}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}\hat{\varphi}\label{eq:-22}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the expression of 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

 given by 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:xi"

\end_inset

, we can write :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
v_{d}=\frac{mv^{2}}{q}\frac{6\pi\rho_{0}^{2}}{\mu_{0}M}\left(2-\frac{\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\sin^{6}\vartheta}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)\right)\frac{\sin^{7}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}\label{eq:-23}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The equatorial distance travelled over a bounce period has the following
 expression :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\rho_{0}\delta\varphi=\tau_{b}\left\langle v_{d}\right\rangle _{\vartheta}=\frac{\tau_{b}}{\pi-2\vartheta_{b}}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\pi-\vartheta_{b}}v_{d}\left(\vartheta,\xi_{0}\right)d\vartheta\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can deduce the expression of the drift time scale, using the expression
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{d}$
\end_inset

 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\tau_{d}=\frac{2\pi}{\delta\varphi}\tau_{b}=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{3\rho_{0}}\frac{q}{mv^{2}}\left(\frac{1}{\pi-2\vartheta_{b}}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\pi-\vartheta_{b}}\left(2-\frac{\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\sin^{6}\vartheta}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)\right)\frac{\sin^{7}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}d\vartheta\right)^{-1}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_{d}=\frac{2\pi}{\delta\varphi}\tau_{b}=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{3\rho_{0}}\frac{q}{mv^{2}}\left(\frac{2}{\pi-2\vartheta_{b}}\int_{\vartheta_{b}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}}\left(2-\frac{\sqrt{1+3\cos^{2}\vartheta}}{\sin^{6}\vartheta}\left(1-\xi_{0}^{2}\right)\right)\frac{\sin^{7}\vartheta}{\left(3\cos^{2}\vartheta+1\right)^{2}}d\vartheta\right)^{-1}\label{eq:taud}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\tau_{d}=\frac{\mu_{0}M}{3\rho_{0}}\frac{q}{mv^{2}}g\left(\xi_{0}\right)}\label{eq:taudg}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 trend is shown in figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:Drift-time-scale"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename taudxi0.eps
	width 100line%

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Caption

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Drift time scale function 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "fig:Drift-time-scale"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Like with the bounce time scale, we normalize the drift time scale with
 respect to its value at 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{0}=0$
\end_inset

.
 This gives :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_{dn}=\frac{\tau_{d}}{\tau_{d}^{*}}=\frac{\tau_{d}}{\frac{\mu_{0}M}{3\rho_{0}}\frac{q}{mv^{2}}g\left(0\right)}=\frac{g\left(\xi_{0}\right)}{g\left(0\right)}\label{eq:taudn}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Bounce time scale and drift time scale comparison
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to say that the bounce and drift time scales are on a different
 ordering level, the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\tau_{b}^{*}}{\tau_{d}^{*}}$
\end_inset

 has to remain arbitrarily small.
 The expression of the ratio reads, using 
\begin_inset Formula $f\left(0\right)=\frac{\pi\sqrt{2}}{6}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g\left(0\right)=1$
\end_inset

 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\boxed{\frac{\tau_{b}^{*}}{\tau_{d}^{*}}=2\pi\sqrt{2}\frac{m}{q\mu_{0}M}\rho_{0}^{2}v=\frac{4\pi}{\mu_{0}M}\frac{m}{q\sqrt{2}}\rho_{0}^{2}v}\label{eq:taubstartaudstar}\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Using the values 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\mu_{0}M}{4\pi}=8.3\times10^{15}Tm^{3}$
\end_inset

 for Earth's magnetic field, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{p}=1832m_{e}=1.67\times10^{-27}kg$
\end_inset

 the mass of a proton and 
\begin_inset Formula $q=1.6\times10^{-19}C$
\end_inset

 the elementary charge, the leading coefficient has the following value
 :
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{4\pi}{\mu_{0}M}\frac{m}{q\sqrt{2}}=\frac{1.67\times10^{-27}}{8.3\times10^{15}\times1.6\times10^{-19}\sqrt{2}}=8.89\times10^{-25}sm^{-3}\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Evolution of the ratio with respect to the particle distance and speed is
 shown on figure 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "fig:taubtaud"

\end_inset

.
 We can see that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{0}\lesssim2R_{T}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\tau_{b}^{*}}{\tau_{d}^{*}}<10$
\end_inset

 for all velocities (the particle obviously can't travel faster than the
 speed of light).
 This ensures that the separation of the time scales is consistent for all
 velocities within a radius of 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{0p}\simeq2R_{T}\simeq10^{4}km$
\end_inset

 from the center of the Earth.
 Beyond this threshold, particles with enough velocity will have comparable
 bounce and drift time scale and the assumption will not hold.
 It is to note that the calculation is done for a proton.
 Since the ratio depends linearly on the particle mass, the electron ordering
 threshold is 1832 times higher, i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{0e}\simeq4\times10^{3}R_{T}\simeq2\times10^{7}km$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

